Task 1: Enable Distributed Tracing with Jaeger
Subtask 1.1: Verify Service Mesh Installation
First, let's verify that Istio service mesh is properly installed and running.

# Check if Istio system namespace exists
kubectl get namespace istio-system

# Verify Istio components are running
kubectl get pods -n istio-system

# Check if the service mesh control plane is ready
kubectl get servicemeshcontrolplane -n istio-system
Expected output should show all Istio components in Running status.

Subtask 1.2: Install Jaeger Operator
Install the Jaeger operator which will manage Jaeger instances in your cluster.

# Create the Jaeger operator namespace
kubectl create namespace observability

# Apply the Jaeger operator subscription
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: jaeger-product
  namespace: observability
spec:
  channel: stable
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: jaeger-product
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
Wait for the operator to be installed:

# Check operator installation status
kubectl get csv -n observability | grep jaeger

# Verify operator pod is running
kubectl get pods -n observability
Subtask 1.3: Deploy Jaeger Instance
Create a Jaeger instance for collecting and storing traces.

# Create Jaeger instance configuration
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: jaegertracing.io/v1
kind: Jaeger
metadata:
  name: jaeger-small-production
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  strategy: production
  storage:
    type: elasticsearch
    elasticsearch:
      nodeCount: 1
      storage:
        size: 1Gi
      resources:
        requests:
          cpu: 200m
          memory: 1Gi
        limits:
          cpu: 500m
          memory: 2Gi
  collector:
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      limits:
        cpu: 200m
        memory: 256Mi
  query:
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      limits:
        cpu: 200m
        memory: 256Mi
EOF
Verify Jaeger deployment:

# Check Jaeger pods status
kubectl get pods -n istio-system | grep jaeger

# Get Jaeger service information
kubectl get svc -n istio-system | grep jaeger
Subtask 1.4: Configure Service Mesh for Tracing
Update the Istio configuration to enable tracing with Jaeger.

# Create or update the telemetry configuration
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: install.istio.io/v1alpha1
kind: IstioOperator
metadata:
  name: control-plane
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  values:
    pilot:
      traceSampling: 100.0
    global:
      tracer:
        zipkin:
          address: jaeger-small-production-collector.istio-system:9411
  meshConfig:
    defaultConfig:
      tracing:
        sampling: 100.0
        zipkin:
          address: jaeger-small-production-collector.istio-system:9411
EOF
Apply the configuration:

# Restart Istio control plane to pick up new configuration
kubectl rollout restart deployment/istiod -n istio-system

# Wait for rollout to complete
kubectl rollout status deployment/istiod -n istio-system
Task 2: Trace Service Requests Across Microservices
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Sample Application
Deploy the Bookinfo application to demonstrate distributed tracing.

# Create application namespace
kubectl create namespace bookinfo

# Label namespace for Istio injection
kubectl label namespace bookinfo istio-injection=enabled

# Deploy Bookinfo application
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.19/samples/bookinfo/platform/kube/bookinfo.yaml -n bookinfo

# Deploy Bookinfo gateway and virtual service
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.19/samples/bookinfo/networking/bookinfo-gateway.yaml -n bookinfo
Verify application deployment:

# Check pod status
kubectl get pods -n bookinfo

# Verify services are created
kubectl get svc -n bookinfo

# Check gateway configuration
kubectl get gateway -n bookinfo
Subtask 2.2: Configure Application for Tracing
Ensure the application is properly configured to generate traces.

# Create destination rules for load balancing
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: productpage
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  host: productpage
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: reviews
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  host: reviews
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
  - name: v2
    labels:
      version: v2
  - name: v3
    labels:
      version: v3
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: ratings
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  host: ratings
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: details
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  host: details
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Generate Traffic for Tracing
Create traffic to generate traces across the microservices.

# Get the ingress gateway URL
export INGRESS_HOST=$(kubectl get po -l istio=ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.hostIP}')
export INGRESS_PORT=$(kubectl -n istio-system get service istio-ingressgateway -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[?(@.name=="http2")].nodePort}')
export GATEWAY_URL=$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT

# Generate traffic using a loop
for i in {1..50}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i sent"
  sleep 1
done
Alternative method using a load generator:

# Deploy a load generator pod
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: load-generator
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: load-generator
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: load-generator
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: load-generator
        image: busybox
        command: ["/bin/sh"]
        args: ["-c", "while true; do wget -q -O- http://productpage:9080/productpage; sleep 2; done"]
EOF
Subtask 2.4: Access Jaeger UI
Set up access to the Jaeger user interface.

# Create a route to Jaeger query service (OpenShift)
oc expose svc jaeger-small-production-query -n istio-system

# Get the Jaeger UI URL
oc get route jaeger-small-production-query -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}'

# For Kubernetes, use port-forward
kubectl port-forward -n istio-system svc/jaeger-small-production-query 16686:16686
Access the Jaeger UI at: http://localhost:16686 (if using port-forward) or the route URL (if using OpenShift).

Task 3: Identify Latency and Failures in Service-to-Service Requests
Subtask 3.1: Analyze Trace Data
Navigate through the Jaeger UI to analyze traces:

Service Selection: In the Jaeger UI, select productpage.bookinfo from the Service dropdown
Operation Selection: Choose productpage.bookinfo: GET /productpage
Time Range: Set an appropriate time range (last 1 hour)
Find Traces: Click "Find Traces" to display collected traces
Subtask 3.2: Examine Individual Traces
Click on individual traces to examine detailed information:

Key metrics to observe:

Total Duration: Overall request processing time
Span Count: Number of services involved in the request
Service Dependencies: Visual representation of service calls
Subtask 3.3: Introduce Latency for Analysis
Create artificial latency to demonstrate trace analysis capabilities.

# Create a virtual service with delay injection
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews-delay
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        end-user:
          exact: jason
    fault:
      delay:
        percentage:
          value: 100.0
        fixedDelay: 7s
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
EOF
Generate traffic with the delay:

# Send requests with specific header to trigger delay
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s -H "end-user: jason" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Delayed request $i sent"
  sleep 2
done
Subtask 3.4: Introduce Errors for Failure Analysis
Create error conditions to analyze failure traces.

# Create a virtual service with error injection
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: ratings-error
  namespace: bookinfo
spec:
  hosts:
  - ratings
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        end-user:
          exact: jason
    fault:
      abort:
        percentage:
          value: 100.0
        httpStatus: 500
    route:
    - destination:
        host: ratings
        subset: v1
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: ratings
        subset: v1
EOF
Generate error traffic:

# Send requests that will trigger errors
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s -H "end-user: jason" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Error request $i sent"
  sleep 2
done
